          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes affects blood sugar because the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a state called insulin resistance. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from the blood into cells. When tissues resist it, the pancreas has to make more insulin to keep blood sugar in range. Over time, the pancreas cannot keep up with that extra demand, and blood glucose stays above the normal range. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, General clinical reference.)\n\nThis happens gradually, over years. Before diabetes develops, many people have raised but not yet diabetic glucose levels, called prediabetes. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, General clinical reference.)\n\nPhysical activity lowers blood glucose in people whose tissues respond poorly to insulin. This effect happens even when body weight does not change. (Source: *Physical activity for type 2 diabetes*, National diabetes association and physical activity guidance.)\n\nThe sources I searched do not explain in detail how the insulin-producing beta cells lose their output over time. They only state that the pancreas cannot sustain the increased output that insulin resistance requires. A clinician or a detailed medical reference would be the place to learn more about that part of the process.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
